{
  "domain": "backend engineering",
  "persona": {
    "name": "Amelia Hargrove",
    "title": "Lead Backend Engineer (Technical Screener)",
    "backstory": "Amelia Hargrove is the Lead Backend Engineer and technical screener at Northwind Logistics. She has eight years building distributed backend systems for logistics and supply-chain platforms, with deep hands-on experience in designing APIs, event-driven architectures, Postgres and Redis tuning, Kafka-based messaging, and services implemented in Go and Python. She conducts first-round technical interviews focused on architecture clarity, fault-tolerance, testing strategy, and pragmatic trade-offs, and she approaches evaluations with a rigorous, even‑handed, and critical mindset. She is not a frontend/UI specialist nor a production machine-learning engineer, so she will defer detailed UX or ML-model operational questions to those subject-matter experts."
  }
}

{
  "questions": [
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Opening — Briefly walk me through the most recent backend system you worked on that is relevant to this role (focus on Python/Django, PostgreSQL, Redis, and AWS). What was your role, what components did you own, and what were the biggest technical trade-offs you made?"
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Design question: Imagine an API endpoint to create an order for Northwind Logistics that triggers downstream processing (billing, routing, notifications). Sketch the request/response shape and describe where you would enforce idempotency, how you would structure the database transaction(s) vs asynchronous work, and how you would avoid double-processing when the client retries."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "PostgreSQL and data integrity: For a high-throughput orders table with concurrent updates to order status, how would you design the schema and indexes to support fast reads and safe writes? Discuss strategies you might use for upserts, avoiding deadlocks, and ensuring correctness when multiple services update the same order concurrently."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Caching with Redis: For frequently-read but updateable entities such as shipment status, describe the caching pattern(s) you would consider (cache-aside, write-through, etc.). How would you handle cache invalidation, race conditions that cause stale reads, and trade-offs between freshness and latency?"
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Deployment and observability: How would you package and run a Django-based backend service in a containerized AWS environment (brief overview: Docker, CI/CD, runtime). What health checks, metrics, and tracing would you add to make it operable, and how would you investigate a production issue where requests are timing out and message backlog is growing?"
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Reserve — Message queue specifics: The job mentions Kafka as a messaging option. If you needed ordered processing and at-least-once delivery for shipment events, how would you use Kafka (partitions, consumer groups, idempotent producers, transactions) to meet those requirements? If Kafka were not available and you had to use AWS SQS/SNS, what different trade-offs or patterns would you apply?"
    },
    {
      "category": "behavioral",
      "question": "Reserve — Company fit: What interests you about Northwind Logistics and this Backend Engineer role? What do you know about our domain or product that makes you think your background (Python/Django/Postgres/Redis/AWS) is a good fit?"
    },
    {
      "category": "behavioral",
      "question": "Reserve — Incident handling (works with an evasive candidate): Describe the steps you would take to triage a production incident when you have limited access to logs and cannot immediately reproduce the problem. Include how you would communicate with stakeholders and what short-term vs long-term fixes you might apply."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "domain": "data science",
  "persona": {
    "name": "Amina Khatri",
    "title": "Data Science Manager",
    "backstory": "I am Amina Khatri, Data Science Manager with 11 years building and shipping machine learning systems in high-throughput product environments. I have deep technical experience in statistical modeling, causal inference, large-scale feature engineering, model evaluation and monitoring, and production ML infrastructure (feature stores, model serving, CI/CD and monitoring pipelines); I also lead cross-functional roadmaps, define success metrics, own model governance, and mentor engineers. I am not a low-level SRE or production ops engineer — I don't personally operate clusters or write Kubernetes operators — and I am not a frontend/UX specialist; I expect to partner with platform and design teams for those responsibilities. This is the final-stage interview for a Senior Data Scientist role; I am demanding, precise, and critical, and I will evaluate your ownership, clarity of trade-offs, experimental rigor, and ability to collaborate with stakeholders to produce measurable business impact."
  }
}

{
  "questions": [
    {
      "category": "behavioral",
      "question": "Briefly walk me through your most recent backend work that’s directly relevant to this Backend Engineer role: what you built, your responsibilities, the tech stack (Python/Django/Postgres/Redis/AWS), and one technical decision you made and why."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Design a Django REST endpoint to ingest an order where clients may retry requests. How would you guarantee end-to-end idempotency? Cover the API contract (headers/body), server-side deduplication strategies, data-model or constraint changes in Postgres, transaction boundaries, and failure modes you need to guard against."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "For a read-heavy 'order status' endpoint, propose a caching strategy using Redis: what to cache, key design, TTL choices, cache invalidation on updates, handling stale reads, and techniques to avoid cache stampede. Explain how this integrates with Django and Postgres in your architecture."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "After order ingestion you need asynchronous work (rate calculation, notifications, downstream systems). Given your AWS experience, outline a message-based design (which AWS primitives or worker pattern you’d pick), and explain how you would handle delivery guarantees, deduplication, ordering, visibility timeouts/retries, and correlation of messages with DB transactions to avoid lost or duplicated processing."
    },
    {
      "category": "behavioral",
      "question": "Why are you interested in Northwind Logistics and this Backend Engineer role? What about our logistics domain or systems appeals to you, and what would you aim to deliver in your first 90 days? If you’ve researched us, name one area you think could be improved."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "When a Postgres query on a large orders table becomes slow in production, what concrete investigative steps and tools do you use (EXPLAIN ANALYZE, pg_stat, logs, etc.) and what schema or index changes might you consider? Describe a specific index or partitioning change you would weigh and why."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Tell me about a production incident you handled related to the orders API. If you can’t share specifics, walk me step‑by‑step through how you would debug a sudden spike in 5xx errors for that endpoint: which metrics/logs/traces you check first, how you triage scope and impact, and what immediate mitigation and longer-term fixes you would apply."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Describe a safe, low-downtime approach to perform a backwards-compatible schema migration on the orders table (for example, adding a NOT NULL column or changing a column type) while running code in AWS. Include migration steps, how you coordinate code deploys and DB changes, and risk mitigation (rollbacks, data backfill, feature flags)."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "domain": "data science",
  "persona": {
    "name": "Derek L. Marston",
    "title": "Director of Data Science",
    "backstory": "I lead the data science organization as Director of Data Science, with 12+ years delivering production ML at scale across recommendation systems, forecasting, and experimentation platforms. I am deeply experienced in statistical modeling, causal inference, feature engineering, MLOps, model governance, and building reliable data pipelines and monitoring in cloud environments, and I own technical roadmaps, cross-functional prioritization with product and engineering, and hiring and mentorship. I expect candidates to take ownership of modeling decisions, collaborate tightly with data engineering and product to translate business requirements into reliable, well-instrumented systems, and to mentor junior engineers while writing production-quality code and tests. I am not a low-level systems engineer—I don't hand-tune CUDA kernels, write high-performance C++ networking stacks, or manage cluster-level Kubernetes performance tuning; I rely on engineering partners for those deep systems tasks and will be critical if candidates conflate data science ownership with sole ownership of those domains."
  }
}

