{
  "questions": [
    {
      "category": "behavioral",
      "question": "Open the interview: briefly summarize the last 3 years of your hands-on work using Python, SQL, scikit-learn and Spark. Focus on one clear project where you had primary ownership, the technical decisions you made, and the measurable impact — be specific about your role and trade-offs you chose."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "You are asked to build a production churn model for a large subscription product. Walk me through end-to-end design: data ingestion (Spark), feature engineering, training strategy (including imbalance and cross-validation), model choice and why (scikit-learn vs distributed learners), deployment and realtime vs batch scoring, and how you would prevent feature leakage. For each stage explain trade-offs, failure modes, and monitoring you would put in place."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Our stack must scale: describe a concrete workflow to train and validate a complex model on terabytes of data using Spark with scikit-learn components. Include how you would: sample or aggregate data to make training tractable, perform feature transformations reproducibly, handle hyperparameter tuning (what to do if XGBoost is unavailable in your environment), and ensure experiments are reproducible. Call out bottlenecks, cost/performance trade-offs, and how you'd debug failures."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Design an experiment to evaluate a retention (anti-churn) intervention delivered to a subset of users. Define primary and secondary metrics, explain how you compute required sample size and minimum detectable effect, allocation and randomization strategy, how you would detect and correct for imbalance or contamination, and how you would interpret results if different metrics conflict. Include safeguards against common biases and practical rollout/ramp decisions."
    },
    {
      "category": "behavioral",
      "question": "As a senior data scientist you'll need to balance new feature work, model maintenance, and technical debt while working with product and engineering. Give a concrete example from your experience where you prioritized competing requests, negotiated scope with stakeholders, and established ownership for model lifecycle (training, deployment, monitoring). What criteria did you use to decide, and what was the outcome?"
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Feature leakage is a common silent failure. Give specific examples of subtle feature or label leakage you would look for in a churn/retention pipeline built on event logs. How would you detect such leakage during EDA and during model validation, and what automated checks or tests would you add to a Spark-based pipeline to prevent it from reaching production?"
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Walk me through a precise SQL approach (including window functions, joins and indexing strategy) to compute cohort-based 30-day retention and a rolling 30-day retention rate per user cohort. Highlight performance pitfalls on large tables, how you'd optimize for speed in Spark/warehouse, and how you would validate that the SQL logic matches business definitions."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Once a classification model is in production for retention prediction, what monitoring and alerting would you put in place to detect model degradation or data drift? Specify metrics to monitor (both data and model-level), thresholds or detection approaches you would use, diagnostic steps when an alert fires, and an actionable rollback or mitigation plan."
    }
  ]
}

