{
  "domain": "hospitality",
  "persona": {
    "name": "Maya Calderon",
    "title": "Talent Acquisition Partner",
    "backstory": "I’m Maya Calderon, Talent Acquisition Partner at Seaside Resorts with six years of recruiting experience in the hospitality sector, primarily hiring for front office and guest-facing roles. I know well the day-to-day responsibilities of front-desk teams, guest experience priorities, leadership competencies, scheduling, and I have a high-level familiarity with common property-management systems. I do not handle deep technical or engineering questions about system integrations or product development — my role is a first-contact screen focused on fit and motivation. I’m warm and conversational and will focus on your background, behavioral examples, availability, salary expectations, work authorization, and location or shift preferences."
  }
}

{
  "questions": [
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Opening — In one concise answer, walk me through the most impactful end-to-end data science project you led using Python, SQL, scikit-learn and Spark: the business goal, your role and ownership, the data scale and pipeline, the modelling approach, how you validated and deployed the model, and the measurable business outcome."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "At scale in Spark: describe how you would build, validate and productionize a predictive model when the training data does not fit in memory. Explain your choices for feature engineering, data partitioning, cross-validation or holdout strategies, hyperparameter tuning approach, reproducibility, and the main failure modes you would watch for."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Feature leakage prevention: give a concrete checklist and concrete examples of subtle leakage that can occur across SQL joins, time-based aggregations and Spark pipelines. Explain how you would design data splits, instrumentation, and automated tests to detect and prevent leakage before deployment."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Modeling trade-offs and production constraints: given your experience with scikit-learn and Spark, explain how you would decide between: (a) training and serving a scikit-learn pipeline on sampled/engineered features, versus (b) using distributed training in Spark for a full dataset. Cover trade-offs in predictive performance, training time, operational cost, interpretability, model calibration, and rollback strategies."
    },
    {
      "category": "behavioral",
      "question": "Ownership and cross‑functional collaboration: this senior role will own initiatives such as churn modelling. Describe how you would partner with product, engineering, and analytics to define success metrics, deployment and monitoring plans, and incident response. Give an example from your past where you led a cross‑team effort, how you handled disagreements, and what you changed to ensure long‑term ownership."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Reserve — SQL for feature engineering at scale: outline the sequence of SQL operations you would use to compute user‑level rolling features (e.g., 7/30/90-day event counts, recency) from an events table with hundreds of millions of rows. Explain choices to optimize performance (indexes/partitioning/materialized views), and checks to avoid double‑counting or leakage."
    },
    {
      "category": "technical",
      "question": "Reserve — experiment design gap: the role requires A/B testing but you did not list it explicitly. Explain, at a senior level, how you would design an evaluation strategy that combines offline validation with an online randomized experiment for a retention intervention where churn is rare and effects may take weeks to appear. Cover metric selection, power/sample size considerations, interim monitoring, and guardrails to protect production systems."
    },
    {
      "category": "behavioral",
      "question": "Reserve for evasive or difficult candidate — incident and accountability: if a production model you own degrades suddenly and the engineer is evasive about recent changes, what immediate technical and managerial steps do you take? Specify the diagnostics you'd run first, how you'd communicate with stakeholders, and how you'd ensure root cause analysis without creating a blame culture."
    }
  ]
}

